# x9: 20 new guides (part 2)
try: FAQX
except NameError: FAQX={}; METAX={}
LONG.update({
'smart-objects-album-design':('Smart Objects in Photoshop for Album Design',[
"Smart Objects are one of the most useful and least understood features for album designers. They let you scale, rotate and filter photos repeatedly without damaging the original pixels, and they make template reuse far faster. This guide explains what Smart Objects are and how to use them on real album sheets.",
"## What a Smart Object is",
"A Smart Object is a container that holds the original image data inside your PSD. When you transform it, Photoshop recalculates from the original every time instead of from the last resized version. Shrink a photo to 30 percent, change your mind, and scale it back to 100 percent: with a normal layer it turns soft, but with a Smart Object it stays sharp.",
"## Placing photos as Smart Objects",
"Use File > Place Embedded, or drag a file from your folder onto the canvas. Photoshop places it as a Smart Object automatically (check Preferences > General > Always Create Smart Objects when Placing). If a photo is already a normal layer, right-click it and choose Convert to Smart Object.",
"### Embedded or linked?",
"- **Embedded:** the photo is stored inside the PSD. The file is self-contained but larger.",
"- **Linked:** the PSD points to the photo on disk. Files stay smaller, but moving or renaming the photo breaks the link.",
"For albums sent to other designers or archived, embedded is safer. For working files on one machine, linked can keep large 12x36 PSDs manageable.",
"## Smart Objects and clipping masks",
"Smart Objects clip into frame layers just like normal layers. Place the photo above the frame and press Alt+Ctrl+G. You can now move and scale it freely inside the frame. Our [clipping masks guide](/blog/clipping-masks-explained/) covers frame setup in detail.",
"## Smart Filters for non-destructive edits",
"Any filter applied to a Smart Object becomes a Smart Filter, which you can switch off, re-edit or mask later. Useful filters for album work include:",
"- **Camera Raw Filter:** quick exposure, white balance and grading on a placed photo.",
"- **Gaussian Blur:** soften a background photo used behind text.",
"- **High Pass or Unsharp Mask:** gentle sharpening before print.",
"The filter mask lets you paint where the effect applies, for example sharpening eyes but not skin.",
"## Replacing contents to reuse layouts",
"Here is where Smart Objects save real time. Right-click a photo Smart Object and choose Replace Contents, then pick a new photo. The new image takes the position, size, rotation and filters of the old one. Build one master spread, then swap photos for the next wedding in minutes. Combined with [Photoshop actions](/blog/photoshop-actions-for-album-designers/), this turns repetitive work into a few clicks.",
"### When Replace Contents goes wrong",
"If the new photo has a different aspect ratio or resolution, it may appear larger or smaller than expected. Fix it with Free Transform, or keep all source photos at similar pixel dimensions before replacing.",
"## Smart Objects for template elements",
"Template designers often wrap ornaments, frames and textures into Smart Objects too. Double-click the thumbnail to open its contents in a separate document, edit it, save, and the change updates on the sheet. If the same ornament appears several times as copies of one Smart Object, editing one updates all of them.",
"## Performance tips",
"Smart Objects increase file size, especially with large camera files. Before placing, resize photos to roughly the size they will appear on the sheet plus some margin; a photo filling half of a 12x36 spread needs about 5400 pixels wide, not 8000. If files still feel heavy, see [why Photoshop slows down with large album PSDs](/blog/photoshop-slow-large-album-psd/).",
"## Rasterising before export",
"You do not need to rasterise Smart Objects before exporting a JPG; the export flattens everything. Keep your layered PSD intact for future edits and read [how to export album sheets as JPG for print](/blog/export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print/).",
"## Practise on free templates",
"Try these techniques on a sheet from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/), a [12x18 album sheet](/category/album-12x18/) or a [studio background PSD](/category/studio-background/). Then follow our [step-by-step album PSD editing guide](/blog/how-to-edit-wedding-album-psd/).",
]),
'photoshop-actions-for-album-designers':('Photoshop Actions for Album Designers',[
"Album design involves the same small tasks again and again: resizing photos, adding guides, sharpening, saving JPGs. Photoshop actions record those steps once and replay them with a click or a function key. This guide shows which actions album designers find most useful and how to build them safely.",
"## How actions work",
"An action is a recorded sequence of commands. Open the Actions panel (Window > Actions), create a new set to keep your actions organised, then create a new action, assign an optional function key, and press Record. Every command you perform is saved until you click Stop. Play the action later and Photoshop repeats the steps.",
"### What actions cannot record",
"Brush strokes, some tool movements and manual selections are not recorded reliably. Actions work best with menu commands, adjustment layers, dialog settings and file operations.",
"## Five actions worth building",
"### 1. New album sheet with guides",
"Record File > New at your standard size, for example 36 x 12 inches at 300 DPI, then View > Guides > New Guide Layout for margins and a centre line. One key press now gives a correctly set up 12x36 sheet. See the [12x36 album size guide](/blog/12x36-album-size-guide/) for exact dimensions.",
"### 2. Prepare photo for placement",
"Record Image > Image Size to a long edge of about 5400 pixels, convert to sRGB with Edit > Convert to Profile, and save. Run this on originals before placing so album PSDs stay light.",
"### 3. Clip and convert",
"With a photo layer selected above a frame, record Convert to Smart Object followed by Create Clipping Mask. It saves two steps per photo, which adds up over forty spreads.",
"### 4. Print sharpening",
"Record a stamp-visible layer (Ctrl+Alt+Shift+E), a High Pass filter at about 1.5 to 2 pixels, and set the layer to Soft Light at 50 percent opacity. Run it just before export.",
"### 5. Flatten and save JPG",
"Record Flatten Image, Save As JPG at quality 12, then step back in History or close without saving the flattened PSD. Be careful: test this on a copy so you never overwrite a layered master.",
"## Batch processing whole folders",
"File > Automate > Batch runs an action on every file in a folder. Choose the action, the source folder and a separate destination folder, and tick Override Action Save As Commands if the action contains a save step. This is ideal for exporting forty finished sheets to JPG overnight; our [export guide](/blog/export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print/) covers naming and settings.",
"### Image Processor as an alternative",
"File > Scripts > Image Processor converts a folder to JPG at a chosen size and quality without writing an action. It is simpler for straightforward exports.",
"## Making actions robust",
"- **Use percentages or fixed sizes carefully:** an action that resizes to 50 percent behaves differently on different photos.",
"- **Insert stops:** use Insert Stop to pause for decisions, such as choosing which photo to place.",
"- **Toggle dialogs:** click the dialog icon next to a step to make Photoshop show its settings during playback.",
"- **Save your set:** export the action set as an .atn file and back it up with your fonts and templates.",
"## Combining actions with templates",
"Actions shine when every template follows the same layer naming. If you build your own library from free sheets, rename groups consistently as Background, Frames, Photos and Text. Pair this with [Smart Objects and Replace Contents](/blog/smart-objects-album-design/) and our [workflow speed tips](/blog/speed-up-album-design-workflow/) for a fast, repeatable process.",
"## Keyboard shortcuts still matter",
"Actions handle sequences; shortcuts handle single commands. Learn the core ones in [25 Photoshop shortcuts for album design](/blog/photoshop-shortcuts-album-design/).",
"## Practise on real sheets",
"Download a few sheets from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) and the [album background category](/category/album-background/), and test your actions on copies before using them on client work.",
]),
'export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print':('Export Album Sheets as JPG for Print',[
"After hours of design, the export step decides how the album actually prints. A wrong colour profile, low quality setting or confusing file names can undo good work. This guide covers the export settings, naming and checks that keep album sheets print-ready.",
"## Keep a layered master",
"Always save the finished sheet as a layered PSD first. The JPG is a delivery copy; the PSD is what you return to for corrections. Store both in a clear folder structure, for example ClientName/PSD and ClientName/Print_JPG.",
"## The right export settings",
"### Format and quality",
"JPG at maximum quality (12 in Save As, or 100 in Export As) is the standard for most labs. Some labs accept TIFF for the best quality, but files are much larger. Never use Save for Web on print files; it is designed for small screen images.",
"### Resolution",
"Check Image > Image Size before export. A 12x36 sheet should be 10800 x 3600 pixels at 300 pixels per inch. Export As can strip resolution metadata; File > Save a Copy as JPG keeps it, which some lab software expects.",
"### Colour profile",
"Most Indian labs print from sRGB JPGs. Convert with Edit > Convert to Profile > sRGB IEC61966-2.1 and tick Embed Colour Profile when saving. Do not convert to CMYK unless the lab explicitly asks; our [RGB vs CMYK guide](/blog/rgb-vs-cmyk-for-albums/) explains why.",
"### Bit depth",
"JPG supports only 8 bits per channel. If you edited in 16-bit, Photoshop converts automatically, but gradients can band. Add 0.5 to 1 percent noise to large gradient areas before export to prevent visible steps.",
"## Naming files in page order",
"Labs print in file name order, so use numbers with leading zeros: 00_Cover, 01_Sheet, 02_Sheet up to 40_Sheet. Avoid spaces, special characters and names like final_final2.jpg. Include the client name or order number in the folder name rather than every file.",
"## Exporting many sheets at once",
"For a full album, use File > Scripts > Image Processor. Select the PSD folder, choose JPG at quality 12, tick Convert Profile to sRGB, and set an output folder. Alternatively, build a batch action as described in [Photoshop actions for album designers](/blog/photoshop-actions-for-album-designers/).",
"## Checks before sending",
"- **Open every JPG:** look at each file at fit-to-screen to catch missing photos or hidden layers.",
"- **Check the fold:** no faces or text across the centre of spreads.",
"- **Check edges:** backgrounds extend fully to the edge; text sits inside the safe area.",
"- **Spell names and dates:** read every text element once more.",
"- **Confirm file count:** the number of JPGs matches the agreed sheet count plus cover.",
"Our [print-ready PSD checklist](/blog/print-ready-psd-checklist/) has a fuller list.",
"## Covers need separate attention",
"Covers often have spine and wrap areas, so their dimensions differ from inner sheets. Export them separately and label clearly. See [album cover dimensions](/blog/album-cover-dimensions/) for sizes.",
"## Sending to the lab",
"Zip the folder, name it clearly, and send through the lab's preferred method. Keep the ZIP under the lab's upload limit by splitting if needed. Our guide to [preparing and sending files to the lab](/blog/send-album-files-to-lab/) covers delivery, and [making a PDF for client approval](/blog/wedding-album-pdf-for-client-approval/) covers the approval stage before export.",
"## Practise with free sheets",
"Test your export settings on a sheet from the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) or the [12x30 album category](/category/album-12x30/) and compare a test print with your screen.",
]),
'skin-retouching-for-album-photos':('Skin Retouching for Wedding Album Photos',[
"Album photos are viewed up close and printed large, so skin retouching matters more than on social media. The aim is a clean, natural look that still resembles the person, not plastic skin. This guide covers a fast, non-destructive retouching workflow suited to album volume.",
"## How much retouching is right",
"Retouch for temporary problems, not permanent features. Remove pimples, sweat shine, stray hairs and under-eye shadows from late nights. Leave moles, freckles, laugh lines and natural skin texture unless the client asks otherwise. A good test: would the person recognise themselves instantly?",
"## Prioritise the photos that matter",
"A wedding album may contain 300 photos. Retouch closely only the portraits, couple shots and any face that appears large on a spread. Group photos and small frames need only a quick check. Plan this when you [organise photos before album design](/blog/organise-photos-before-album-design/).",
"## Step 1: Global fixes in Camera Raw or Lightroom",
"Start with exposure, white balance and a gentle reduction in Clarity or Texture on portraits only. The Texture slider at around -10 to -20 smooths skin subtly while keeping detail. Avoid strong negative Clarity, which makes faces look hazy.",
"## Step 2: Spot healing on a separate layer",
"In Photoshop, add an empty layer above the photo. Use the Spot Healing Brush with Sample All Layers ticked to remove blemishes. Keep the brush slightly larger than the spot. Working on a separate layer means you can reduce opacity or erase mistakes later.",
"### Healing Brush and Clone Stamp",
"For blemishes near edges like the hairline or lips, switch to the Healing Brush (Alt-click to sample) or the Clone Stamp at low opacity. Spot healing can drag in unwanted texture in those areas.",
"## Step 3: Even out tones with dodge and burn",
"Create a Curves layer brightened slightly and another darkened slightly, both with black masks. Paint with a soft white brush at low flow on the masks to lighten dark patches and darken shiny spots. This is slower than blurring but keeps natural texture, and it is the method most professional retouchers use.",
"## Step 4: Reduce shine and redness",
"Flash and hot lights create shine on foreheads and noses. Sample a nearby skin tone and paint on a new layer set to Darken mode at 20 to 30 percent opacity. For redness, add a Hue/Saturation layer, target Reds, reduce saturation slightly and mask it to the affected area.",
"## Step 5: Eyes and teeth, gently",
"Brighten eye whites and teeth only a little with a masked Curves layer, and reduce yellow with Hue/Saturation targeting Yellows. Overdone whites look unnatural in print.",
"## Things to avoid",
"- **Heavy blur filters:** surface blur and strong portrait plugins remove pores and look artificial at 12x36 size.",
"- **Changing body shape:** avoid liquify unless the client specifically asks.",
"- **Altering skin colour:** do not lighten skin tone. Respect the natural complexion.",
"## Working with skin in album templates",
"Once photos are placed in templates, check them against the background colour. Warm gold backgrounds can make skin look pale, while cool backgrounds can make it look red. A clipped Color Balance layer on each photo fixes this; see our [colour grading guide](/blog/colour-grading-wedding-album-photos/) and [colour correction tips](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/).",
"## Practise on templates",
"Try retouched portraits in the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) or the [pre-wedding album PSD tag](/tag/pre-wedding-album-psd/), and use the [studio background category](/category/studio-background/) for portrait sheets.",
]),
'fix-missing-fonts-in-psd':('How to Fix Missing Fonts in a PSD File',[
"You open a downloaded album PSD and Photoshop warns that some fonts are missing, or text layers show a yellow triangle. It is one of the most common problems with templates, and it is easy to fix once you know the options. This guide explains why it happens and the safest ways to resolve it.",
"## Why fonts go missing",
"PSD files store text as editable layers that refer to a font by name. The font itself is not embedded. If the template designer used a font you do not have installed, Photoshop cannot render it and shows a warning. The layer still looks correct until you edit it, because Photoshop keeps a preview, but as soon as you type, it switches to a substitute font.",
"## Step 1: Identify the missing fonts",
"Go to Type > Manage Missing Fonts (or Resolve Missing Fonts in older versions). Photoshop lists every font that is not available. You can also select a text layer and look at the font name in the Character panel; missing fonts appear in square brackets.",
"## Step 2: Check the download folder",
"Many template packs include a fonts folder or a text file listing the fonts used. Look inside the ZIP after extracting it. If fonts are included, install them before reopening the PSD.",
"## Step 3: Install the font",
"### On Windows",
"Right-click the .ttf or .otf file and choose Install for all users. Restart Photoshop so it picks up the new font.",
"### On Mac",
"Double-click the font file to open Font Book and click Install. Restart Photoshop.",
"Only install fonts from trustworthy sources, and check the licence; many free fonts allow personal use only, while client albums are commercial work.",
"## Step 4: Use Adobe Fonts if available",
"If you have a Creative Cloud subscription, Photoshop may offer to activate missing fonts from Adobe Fonts automatically. Accept, and the text renders correctly with a licence that covers commercial use.",
"## Step 5: Replace with a similar font",
"If you cannot find the original font, choose a replacement in Manage Missing Fonts. Pick something close in style: a script for a script, a serif for a serif. After replacing, check every text layer because spacing and size can change. Our guide to [choosing fonts for wedding albums](/blog/fonts-for-wedding-albums/) lists dependable font styles.",
"## Fixing layout after substitution",
"Replacement fonts often run wider or narrower. Adjust tracking in the Character panel, or scale the text slightly with Free Transform. For names inside ornaments, keep them centred by aligning to the ornament layer with the Move tool alignment buttons.",
"## Tamil, Telugu and other Indian scripts",
"Templates with Indian-language text need Unicode fonts and the right text engine. Go to Preferences > Type and select the World-Ready Layout (or Middle Eastern and South Asian) option, then restart. Without it, conjuncts and vowel signs may display incorrectly even when the font is installed.",
"## Preventing the problem in your own templates",
"- **Keep a fonts folder:** store every font you use in a backed-up folder with your templates.",
"- **Limit your font list:** two or three dependable pairs cover most albums.",
"- **Convert final text only if needed:** if you share a PSD for printing, you can rasterise or convert text to shapes on a copy so it prints correctly anywhere.",
"## Related help",
"If the PSD will not open at all, see [how to fix ZIP extraction errors](/blog/how-to-fix-zip-extraction-errors/) and [how to download PSD files](/blog/how-to-download-psd-files/). For editing basics, read [how to edit a wedding album PSD](/blog/how-to-edit-wedding-album-psd/). Free templates to practise on are in the [12x36 wedding album category](/category/wedding-album-12x36/) and the [wedding invitation category](/category/wedding-invitation/), where text-heavy designs make font handling especially important.",
]),
})
